<!-- COMMON MISTAKES -->
<section class="content" id="common-mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Pitfalls in GMT to SAST Conversions</h2>
  <article>
    <h3>1. Confusing GMT with British Summer Time (BST)</h3>
    <p>
      A frequent error occurs during UK summer months when London observes British Summer Time (BST, UTC+1). During this period, London is 1 hour behind South Africa (SAST, UTC+2). However, standard GMT (UTC+0) remains 2 hours behind SAST year-round. Always clarify whether your UK contact is using GMT or BST.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>2. Expecting Seasonal Clock Shifts in South Africa</h3>
    <p>
      Some travelers incorrectly assume South Africa shifts its clocks for winter or summer. South Africa maintains a constant UTC+2 offset throughout the entire year. Never adjust SAST calculations for daylight saving transitions.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>3. Conflating SAST with East Africa Time (EAT)</h3>
    <p>
      While SAST shares the UTC+2 offset with Central Africa Time (CAT), it is different from East Africa Time (EAT), which operates at UTC+3 (e.g., Kenya, Tanzania, Ethiopia). Conflating these acronyms leads to 1-hour scheduling misalignments across African regional operations.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>4. Date Line Rollover Errors on Late-Night Schedules</h3>
    <p>
      When converting late-night GMT timestamps (such as 11:00 PM GMT), adding 2 hours yields 01:00 AM SAST on the subsequent calendar day. Failing to note the date shift can result in missed virtual appointments.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>5. Server Timezone Database Misconfigurations</h3>
    <p>
      DevOps teams sometimes misconfigure server locale settings as UTC+1 instead of Africa/Johannesburg (UTC+2), causing database cron jobs to fire an hour early.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<!-- TECHNICAL TIMEZONE DATABASE SECTION -->
<section class="content" id="technical-details" aria-labelledby="tech-heading">
  <h2 id="tech-heading">Technical Specifications & IANA Timezone Database Metadata</h2>
  <p>
    For software developers, system administrators, and data engineers, time conversion requires exact IANA database references.
  </p>
  <div class="method-grid">
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Canonical Identifier</h3>
      <p>The primary IANA database key for South Africa is <code>Africa/Johannesburg</code>. Alternative aliases include <code>Africa/Mbabane</code> and <code>Africa/Maseru</code>.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>ISO 8601 Offset Representation</h3>
      <p>SAST is expressed in ISO 8601 string formats as <code>+02:00</code> (e.g., <code>2026-07-30T14:00:00+02:00</code>).</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>POSIX Timezone Specifier</h3>
      <p>The POSIX environment string representation for standard SAST is <code>SAST-2</code> (indicating standard time 2 hours ahead of UTC).</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>NTP Synchronization</h3>
      <p>Network Time Protocol (NTP) servers in South Africa synchronize against atomic clocks maintained by the National Metrology Institute of South Africa (NMISA).</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

